    },
    "excluded_steps": [],
    "format_errors": [],
    "steps": [
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 119,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 120,
        "char_start": 0,
        "effective_step": 1,
        "end_token_index": 35,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 36,
        "step_number": 1,
        "text": "Step 1: Recognize that $\\sin(180^\\circ - x) = \\sin(x)$, which allows us to pair terms in the product for simplification."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 288,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 289,
        "char_start": 122,
        "effective_step": 2,
        "end_token_index": 111,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 112,
        "step_number": 2,
        "text": "Step 2: Pair the angles as follows: $\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ$, $\\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ$, $\\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 120^\\circ$, and $\\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ$."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 624,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 625,
        "char_start": 291,
        "effective_step": 3,
        "end_token_index": 275,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 276,
        "step_number": 3,
        "text": "Step 3: Simplify each pair using the identity $\\sin(180^\\circ - x) = \\sin(x)$:\n- $\\sin 160^\\circ = \\sin(180^\\circ - 20^\\circ) = \\sin 20^\\circ$\n- $\\sin 140^\\circ = \\sin(180^\\circ - 40^\\circ) = \\sin 40^\\circ$\n- $\\sin 120^\\circ = \\sin(180^\\circ - 60^\\circ) = \\sin 60^\\circ$\n- $\\sin 100^\\circ = \\sin(180^\\circ - 80^\\circ) = \\sin 80^\\circ$"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 720,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 721,
        "char_start": 627,
        "effective_step": 4,
        "end_token_index": 320,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 321,
        "step_number": 4,
        "text": "Step 4: The product becomes $\\sin^2 20^\\circ \\sin^2 40^\\circ \\sin^2 60^\\circ \\sin^2 80^\\circ$."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 862,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 863,
        "char_start": 723,
        "effective_step": 5,
        "end_token_index": 383,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 384,
        "step_number": 5,
        "text": "Step 5: Use the known value of $\\sin 60^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, so $\\sin^2 60^\\circ = \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{3}{4}$."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1279,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1280,
        "char_start": 865,
        "effective_step": 6,
        "end_token_index": 544,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 545,
        "step_number": 6,
        "text": "Step 6: Apply the product-to-sum identities or known product formulas for sines. For simplicity, use the identity for the product of sines of angles in arithmetic progression:\n\\[\n\\sin x \\sin (60^\\circ - x) \\sin (60^\\circ + x) = \\frac{1}{4} \\sin 3x\n\\]\nFor $x = 20^\\circ$, we have:\n\\[\n\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ = \\frac{1}{4} \\sin 60^\\circ = \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}\n\\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1583,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1584,
        "char_start": 1282,
        "effective_step": 7,
        "end_token_index": 695,
        "near_end": true,
        "prefix_token_count": 696,
        "step_number": 7,
        "text": "Step 7: Since $\\sin^2 20^\\circ \\sin^2 40^\\circ \\sin^2 80^\\circ = \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}\\right)^2 = \\frac{3}{64}$, and including $\\sin^2 60^\\circ = \\frac{3}{4}$, the full product is:\n\\[\n\\sin^2 20^\\circ \\sin^2 40^\\circ \\sin^2 60^\\circ \\sin^2 80^\\circ = \\frac{3}{64} \\cdot \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{9}{256}\n\\]"
      }
    ]
  },
