  "raw_text": "Step 1: Recognize that $\\sin(180^\\circ - x) = \\sin(x)$, which allows us to pair terms in the product for simplification.\n\nStep 2: Pair the angles as follows: $\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ$, $\\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ$, $\\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 120^\\circ$, and $\\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ$.\n\nStep 3: Simplify each pair using the identity $\\sin(180^\\circ - x) = \\sin(x)$:\n- $\\sin 160^\\circ = \\sin(180^\\circ - 20^\\circ) = \\sin 20^\\circ$\n- $\\sin 140^\\circ = \\sin(180^\\circ - 40^\\circ) = \\sin 40^\\circ$\n- $\\sin 120^\\circ = \\sin(180^\\circ - 60^\\circ) = \\sin 60^\\circ$\n- $\\sin 100^\\circ = \\sin(180^\\circ - 80^\\circ) = \\sin 80^\\circ$\n\nStep 4: The product becomes $\\sin^2 20^\\circ \\sin^2 40^\\circ \\sin^2 60^\\circ \\sin^2 80^\\circ$.\n\nStep 5: Use the known value of $\\sin 60^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, so $\\sin^2 60^\\circ = \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{3}{4}$.\n\nStep 6: Apply the product-to-sum identities or known product formulas for sines. For simplicity, use the identity for the product of sines of angles in arithmetic progression:\n\\[\n\\sin x \\sin (60^\\circ - x) \\sin (60^\\circ + x) = \\frac{1}{4} \\sin 3x\n\\]\nFor $x = 20^\\circ$, we have:\n\\[\n\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ = \\frac{1}{4} \\sin 60^\\circ = \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}\n\\]\n\nStep 7: Since $\\sin^2 20^\\circ \\sin^2 40^\\circ \\sin^2 80^\\circ = \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}\\right)^2 = \\frac{3}{64}$, and including $\\sin^2 60^\\circ = \\frac{3}{4}$, the full product is:\n\\[\n\\sin^2 20^\\circ \\sin^2 40^\\circ \\sin^2 60^\\circ \\sin^2 80^\\circ = \\frac{3}{64} \\cdot \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{9}{256}\n\\]\n\nFinal answer: $\\boxed{\\frac{9}{256}}$<|im_end|>",
